          "role": "assistant",
          "content": "No. JavaScript’s default `Array.prototype.sort()` converts elements to strings and sorts lexicographically, not numerically.\n\n```js\nconst arr = [11, 2, 1, 20];\n\nconsole.log(arr.sort());\n// [1, 11, 2, 20]\n```\n\nCorrect numeric sort:\n\n```js\nconst arr = [11, 2, 1, 20];\n\nconsole.log(arr.sort((a, b) => a - b));\n// [1, 2, 11, 20]\n```\n\nSo:\n\n- Default: `[11, 2, 1, 20].sort()` → `[1, 11, 2, 20]`\n- Numeric: `[11, 2, 1, 20].sort((a, b) => a - b)` → `[1, 2, 11, 20]`",
